tips on properly explaining mousepad characteristics:

# # surface texture
- abrasive / moderately abrasive / smooth - here we're describing the actual weave used. like the most popular abrasive ones are jacquard and amundsen (though sometimes you could call them moderately abrasive too)
- skin and sleeve friendliness - how stuff glides, whether it catches on your sleeve and so on
- adhesion - your arm can practically stick to some fast smooth pads - stop in style and stuff
![dis is hien, dis is jacquard ^](/pictures/Peripheral/k83_texture.png)

# # friction forces
## ## dynamic friction (glide)
- resistance when the mouse is already moving
- the lower it is, the easier it keeps flying after you get it going

![](/pictures/Peripheral/cisapadv2.png)
GOTTA FIX THE VIDEO LINK RIGHT HERE!!!!!!!!!!! remind me pls

## ## static friction (stopping)
- resistance when you start moving the mouse
- the higher it is, the easier it is to stop the mouse, but harder to get moving
![](/pictures/Peripheral/mousepad_friciton_test.png)

# # glide character
- muddy feel: high static + low dynamic friction == the mouse gets bogged down at the start, but glides pretty well afterwards
- consistent glide: minimal difference between static and dynamic friction
![](/pictures/Peripheral/old_mousepad.png)

# # speed rating (roughly)
- control: high static and dynamic resistance, often muddy, but not always, static clicking meta
- **mid**: moderate static and dynamic resistance, works for everything
- **speed**: low resistance, tracking heavy

*you can play anything on any pad if you want to and have enough skill, it's about general comfort and preference*

# # Balance across x/y axes
- whether there's a difference between moving left-right and up-down
- ideally measure the x/y ratio (even cm/sec down a slope will do)

*rare weaves like artisan k83 can have a nearly identical x/y ratio, but diagonal movements can differ*

# # durability
**moisture resistance:**
- a moisture absorption test usually reflects how quickly the pad will get dirty from sweat and grime in general, so it's worth doing.

![](/pictures/Peripheral/mat_water_resist.png)

# # what to wash it with
- some pads are picky about cleaning products

![](/pictures/Peripheral/ins.gif)

# # backing (base)
**firmness:**
- **xsoft**: squishes a lot, you can push it almost down to the desk
- **soft**: springy give you can feel
- **mid**: firm support, barely gives at all

![](/pictures/Peripheral/xsoft.png)

# # surface makeup
- weave type (tight, jacquard, hybrid, cordura, textured)
- material and edge stitching quality
- base material and how well it grips the desk

![](/pictures/Peripheral/raiden.png)

# # comparative analysis
- comparison with other popular models (artisan, lgg, mgg, x-raypad)
- similarities in:
  - speed and glide character
  - how the texture feels
  - overall experience using it
- like: "similar to artisan raiden, but a little slower" or "feels like g-sr-se, but not as muddy"

# # extra tips:
- test with different mouse skates for the full picture, it can turn out that
  - skates A are faster on pad X and slower on pad Y
  - skates B are faster on pad Y and slower on pad X
- glide character over time: for example some pads *smooth out* after a week and feel different
- what it suits best (arena shooters or angle-holding ones. Better for tracking, switching, clicking or an all-rounder)

